      "title": "Bear Essentials",
      "description": "Bear Essentials – Career Services &  Internship Program | Bridgewater State University BSU and Career Services & Internships will be closed on Monday, September 7th for the Labor Day holiday. New: AI Search is live — ask any career question and get instant answers from our resources. Just click on the \"U\" message icon in the bottom right corner. Log in for personalized results. Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Search Keywords Log in Sign up Guests Access Handshake Schedule an Appointment Request a Workshop Customize Your Experience Who You Are Students Undergraduate Students Graduate Students Non-Degree Students Transfer Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents & Families Prospective Students & Guests Affinities/Identities Asian, Pacific Islanders, & Native American Black/African American First-Generation International Students Latine LGBTQIA+ Students With Disabilities Undocumented Students Veterans Women Career Interests Aviation Business, Accounting and Finance Communications, Marketing and Media Counseling & Human Services Education Fine & Performing Arts Health Care and Wellness Public Administration, Government, and Law STEM (Science, Technology, Engineering & Mathematics) Technology and Cybersecurity Undeclared Students/Explore Career Options Career Resources Bear Essentials Career Readiness: What Employers Are Looking For Cooperative Education (Co-op) Program Create a Resume or Cover Letter Digital Content Library Expand Your Network Explore Your Interests / Self Assessment Find an Internship Internship Funding Make the Most of a Career Fair Negotiate an Offer Prepare for an Interview Prepare for Graduate School Professional Development Funding Search for a Job Signature Internship Programs Design Your Career at BSU Recruit BSU Talent Employer Services HireLocally Job Postings/Handshake Career Fairs & Events Branding Opportunities Internship Information for Employers Internship FAQs CUBEs Industry Mentors Program Recruiting Policies Support #BSU Works About Us Mission & Memberships CSI Reports Staff Location, Parking and Social Media Schedule Appointments Vendor/Resource Requests Career Outcomes Bear Essentials Labor Market Insights Featured Articles Career Resources Career Resources Jobs & Internships Labor Market Insights Alumni Connections Gain Experience Career Outcomes Featured Articles Clubs & Associations Career Videos Bear Essentials Bear Essentials is a free professional clothing closet designed to support students in their career journeys. Whether students need attire for job interviews, internships, research presentations, or any professional setting, Bear Essentials offers a diverse selection of high-quality clothing to help students present their best selves. By providing access to professional attire, this initiative empowers students to boost their confidence and make a lasting impression. How to Access the Professional Outfit Closet: Visit the Career Services & Internships Office: Head to the Rondileau Student Union, Room 104, anytime between 9 a.m. and 4:30 p.m. to access the closet. Remember to bring your connect card! Explore the Closet : Enjoy a 30-minute time slot to browse and pick out articles of clothing to create one professional outfit. Keep Your Outfit : Whatever outfit you choose is yours to keep, completely free of charge! Check Out : Once you’re finished, return to the Career Services & Internships office to complete the check-out process. Safe Space: Bear Essentials aims to provide a diverse selection of professional and business-casual clothing in various shapes, sizes, and styles. We encourage students to choose clothing that reflects their personal style. Dress for Success: Dress for Success is a series of pop-up Bear Essentials clothing events around campus. During this event, students can also receive a complimentary haircut courtesy of BSUPD Barber Partners. To learn about future pop-ups, follow @BSUCareers. Get Involved: Bear Essentials Professional Clothing closet is primarily made up of donations from employers, alumni, and BSU staff and faculty. Donate We are accepting donations for new or gently worn professional clothing, including blouses, blazers, skirts, pants, cardigans, suits, ties, shirts, dresses, and shoes. Tax slips available upon request. Hold a Clothing Drive Looking to have your company make a difference for a BSU Bear? Hold a company clothing drive! Career Services & Internship staff members will then coordinate a time to pick up the clothing from your organization if local. Marketing materials are available upon request. Volunteer Volunteer at one of our Dress for Success events or assist with donations during our annual clothing drive. Students, staff, alumni, and employer partners may patriate in this imitative. Interested in getting involved? Email Katie Muldoon at Kmuldoon@bridgew.edu Skip filter options to posts Featured Articles Filter Clear Filter: Filters See More Filters Filter Controls Blog Categories: Alumni Stories Career & Professional Development Advice Employer Spotlights Front Page News Guest Blog Industry Insights Student Stories Close Clear Filters Introducing the NEW Bear Essentials Closet Share This: Share Introducing the NEW Bear Essentials Closet    on Facebook Share Introducing the NEW Bear Essentials Closet    on LinkedIn Share Introducing the NEW Bear Essentials Closet    on X Copy Link Attention BSU Students! Are you gearing up for an internship or job interview and feeling the pressure to look your best? Look no further—our brand-new Bear Essentials Closet is here to help you shine! What is the Bear Essentials Closet? … By Katie Muldoon Show author info Katie Muldoon Marketing and Program Coordinator Send Email Read more » Sidebar Follow Here For More Career Content! Featured Articles Introducing the NEW Bear Essentials Closet View All Contact & Location Phone 508-531-1328 Email careerservices@bridgew.edu Website https://careers.bridgew.edu/ Address Rondileau Student Union (RSU), Room 104 19 Park Avenue Bridgewater, MA 02325 Office Hours M Monday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm T Tuesday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm W Wednesday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm TH Thursday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm F Friday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm Phone 508-531-1328 Email careerservices@bridgew.edu Website https://careers.bridgew.edu/ Address Rondileau Student Union (RSU), Room 104 19 Park Avenue Bridgewater, MA 02325 Instagram LinkedIn YouTube Footer Column 1 Alumni Connections Blog Events Footer Column 2 Jobs & Internships Resources Meet the Team Privacy Policy | Terms of Service Copyright © 2026 Bridgewater State University"
    },
    "https://careers.bridgew.edu/channels/career-readiness-what-employers-are-looking-for/": {
      "title": "Career Readiness: What Employers Are Looking For",
      "description": "Career Readiness: What Employers Are Looking For – Career Services &  Internship Program | Bridgewater State University BSU and Career Services & Internships will be closed on Monday, September 7th for the Labor Day holiday. New: AI Search is live — ask any career question and get instant answers from our resources. Just click on the \"U\" message icon in the bottom right corner. Log in for personalized results. Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Search Keywords Log in Sign up Guests Access Handshake Schedule an Appointment Request a Workshop Customize Your Experience Who You Are Students Undergraduate Students Graduate Students Non-Degree Students Transfer Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents & Families Prospective Students & Guests Affinities/Identities Asian, Pacific Islanders, & Native American Black/African American First-Generation International Students Latine LGBTQIA+ Students With Disabilities Undocumented Students Veterans Women Career Interests Aviation Business, Accounting and Finance Communications, Marketing and Media Counseling & Human Services Education Fine & Performing Arts Health Care and Wellness Public Administration, Government, and Law STEM (Science, Technology, Engineering & Mathematics) Technology and Cybersecurity Undeclared Students/Explore Career Options Career Resources Bear Essentials Career Readiness: What Employers Are Looking For Cooperative Education (Co-op) Program Create a Resume or Cover Letter Digital Content Library Expand Your Network Explore Your Interests / Self Assessment Find an Internship Internship Funding Make the Most of a Career Fair Negotiate an Offer Prepare for an Interview Prepare for Graduate School Professional Development Funding Search for a Job Signature Internship Programs Design Your Career at BSU Recruit BSU Talent Employer Services HireLocally Job Postings/Handshake Career Fairs & Events Branding Opportunities Internship Information for Employers Internship FAQs CUBEs Industry Mentors Program Recruiting Policies Support #BSU Works About Us Mission & Memberships CSI Reports Staff Location, Parking and Social Media Schedule Appointments Vendor/Resource Requests Career Outcomes Career Readiness: What Employers Are Looking For Labor Market Insights Featured Articles Career Resources Career Resources Jobs & Internships Labor Market Insights Alumni Connections Gain Experience Career Outcomes Featured Articles Clubs & Associations Career Videos Career readiness is the combination of skills, experiences, and behaviors that show employers you’re prepared to contribute, grow, and adapt in a professional environment. No matter your major or career interests, developing these skills is essential — and employers consistently rank them as more important than specific job titles or previous roles. Career readiness is not about having the “perfect job or planning for a specific position.” It’s about being able to articulate what you’ve learned , how you work , and how your experiences have prepared you for the opportunity you’re pursuing. Why Career Readiness Matters Employers want to hire candidates who can learn quickly, collaborate effectively, communicate with clarity, and solve problems with confidence. When you understand and communicate your career readiness skills, you show that you can: Adapt to new environments Contribute as a dependable team member Think critically and solve problems Interact professionally with colleagues and clients Manage your time and responsibilities Keep learning and growing on the job Career readiness gives employers confidence that you’ll thrive — regardless of the setting. Career Readiness Competencies Employers Value Backed up robust data from hundreds of employers, the National Association of Colleges and Employers (NACE) has identified eight core competencies that define what it means to be career-ready. These competencies align closely with the skills students are already developing in the classroom and through co-curricular activities. NACE’s 8 Career Readiness Competencies : Career & Self-Development Communication Critical Thinking Equity & Inclusion Leadership Professionalism Teamwork Technology Every Experience Counts Career readiness isn’t limited to traditional jobs or internships. Employers value any experience where you learned, contributed, solved challenges, or collaborated with others. Your skills can come from: Part-time or full-time jobs Internships or co-ops Research experiences Student clubs or leadership roles Volunteering or community engagement Classroom assignments or capstone projects Study abroad or international experiences Personal projects or entrepreneurial work What matters most is what you did , how you did it , and what you learned . How to Articulate Your Skills Employers connect most with short, clear examples that show your skills in action. A helpful method for telling these stories is the STAR & Reflection approach: STAR Method Situation: What was the context or challenge? Task: What needed to be done? Action: What steps did you take? Result: What happened because of your work? Reflection Add one–two sentences that highlight: What you learned A strength you demonstrated How it prepares you for your future goals Reflection helps employers see your growth, self-awareness, and potential. Examples of STAR Stories From Different Experiences Student Club Leadership You noticed declining club attendance and wanted to improve engagement. You partnered with multiple student organizations, redesigned your promotional strategy, and organized a speaker series. As a result, attendance increased significantly. You learned valuable lessons about teamwork, outreach, and event coordination. Class Project/CUBEs Your team needed to design a prototype for a real client. You organized the project workflow, led testing, and helped translate client needs into technical requirements. The project was successfully implemented. This experience strengthened your problem-solving and project management skills. Volunteer Experience A local community center was struggling to keep volunteers engaged. You created a simple training process, improved communication channels, and coordinated outreach. Attendance and retention improved. You developed meaningful communication, empathy, and leadership skills. Showing Career Readiness on Your Resume or LinkedIn Use action verbs and focus on the impact of your work Include results or numbers when possible (improved, reduced, increased, supported) Connect your examples to the skills employers want Highlight relevant tools, technologies, or methods you’ve used Add projects, presentations, or portfolio items to your LinkedIn profile Your goal is to show a pattern of impact — not just a list of tasks. Translating Common Student Experiences When Connecting with Employers Retail or Customer Service “Provided customer support to over 100 customers weekly, resolved issues, and maintained accuracy during peak hours.” Research Assistant “Collected and analyzed data, summarized findings, and presented insights to project supervisors.” Study Abroad “Adapted to new environments, communicated across cultural differences, and collaborated with international peers on group projects.” Group Projects “Coordinated tasks, facilitated communication, and delivered high-quality work under deadlines.” Keep In Mind Career readiness is about recognizing your own growth and being able to communicate it confidently. You already have more experience and transferable skills than you realize. When you learn to articulate your stories clearly, you show employers that you’re prepared, adaptable, and ready to contribute from day one. Skip filter options to posts Featured Articles Filter Clear Filter: Filters See More Filters Filter Controls Blog Categories: Alumni Stories Career & Professional Development Advice Employer Spotlights Front Page News Guest Blog Industry Insights Student Stories Close Clear Filters One Goal for This Semester: Build Your Career Readiness Share This: Share One Goal for This Semester: Build Your Career Readiness on Facebook Share One Goal for This Semester: Build Your Career Readiness on LinkedIn Share One Goal for This Semester: Build Your Career Readiness on X Copy Link With the new semester underway, comes new experiences, and new opportunities to learn, grow, and prepare for your future. Whether you’re just beginning your first year at BSU or set to graduate this semester, developing your career readiness is an  … By Anastasia Krueger Show author info Anastasia Krueger Early Career Engagement Advisor Send Email Read more » CareerSpring: A Resource that Expands Support for First-Generation Students Share This: Share CareerSpring: A Resource that Expands Support for First-Generation Students on Facebook Share CareerSpring: A Resource that Expands Support for First-Generation Students on LinkedIn Share CareerSpring: A Resource that Expands Support for First-Generation Students on X Copy Link Launching your career is a whole new journey and you don’t have to travel it alone. At Bridgewater State University, first-generation and/or low-income (FGLI) students have access to dedicated support through the Career Services & Internships office, including career advising,  … By Katie Vagen Show author info Katie Vagen Director of Career Development Send Email Read more » Page 1 Page 2 Page 3 … Page 6 Next page » Sidebar Follow Here For More Career Content! Featured Articles One Goal for This Semester: Build Your Career Readiness CareerSpring: A Resource that Expands Support for First-Generation Students Transitioning from College to Corporate: Your Late-Summer Prep Checklist Faculty Are Key to Student Career Success Nora Sperling ’26’s Journey to Meaningful Work at BIDMC View All Contact & Location Phone 508-531-1328 Email careerservices@bridgew.edu Website https://careers.bridgew.edu/ Address Rondileau Student Union (RSU), Room 104 19 Park Avenue Bridgewater, MA 02325 Office Hours M Monday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm T Tuesday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm W Wednesday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm TH Thursday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm F Friday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm Phone 508-531-1328 Email careerservices@bridgew.edu Website https://careers.bridgew.edu/ Address Rondileau Student Union (RSU), Room 104 19 Park Avenue Bridgewater, MA 02325 Instagram LinkedIn YouTube Footer Column 1 Alumni Connections Blog Events Footer Column 2 Jobs & Internships Resources Meet the Team Privacy Policy | Terms of Service Copyright © 2026 Bridgewater State University"
