    "https://careers.bridgew.edu/careerdesign/": {
      "title": "Design Your Career",
      "description": "Design Your Career at BSU – Career Services &  Internship Program | Bridgewater State University BSU and Career Services & Internships will be closed on Monday, September 7th for the Labor Day holiday. New: AI Search is live — ask any career question and get instant answers from our resources. Just click on the \"U\" message icon in the bottom right corner. Log in for personalized results. Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Search Keywords Log in Sign up Guests Access Handshake Schedule an Appointment Request a Workshop Customize Your Experience Who You Are Students Undergraduate Students Graduate Students Non-Degree Students Transfer Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents & Families Prospective Students & Guests Affinities/Identities Asian, Pacific Islanders, & Native American Black/African American First-Generation International Students Latine LGBTQIA+ Students With Disabilities Undocumented Students Veterans Women Career Interests Aviation Business, Accounting and Finance Communications, Marketing and Media Counseling & Human Services Education Fine & Performing Arts Health Care and Wellness Public Administration, Government, and Law STEM (Science, Technology, Engineering & Mathematics) Technology and Cybersecurity Undeclared Students/Explore Career Options Career Resources Bear Essentials Career Readiness: What Employers Are Looking For Cooperative Education (Co-op) Program Create a Resume or Cover Letter Digital Content Library Expand Your Network Explore Your Interests / Self Assessment Find an Internship Internship Funding Make the Most of a Career Fair Negotiate an Offer Prepare for an Interview Prepare for Graduate School Professional Development Funding Search for a Job Signature Internship Programs Design Your Career at BSU Recruit BSU Talent Employer Services HireLocally Job Postings/Handshake Career Fairs & Events Branding Opportunities Internship Information for Employers Internship FAQs CUBEs Industry Mentors Program Recruiting Policies Support #BSU Works About Us Mission & Memberships CSI Reports Staff Location, Parking and Social Media Schedule Appointments Vendor/Resource Requests Career Outcomes Design Your Career at BSU Education + Skills + Experience = Career Confidence. Grounded in Life Design principles, Career Design is a multi-step process that helps you build confidence as you work through questions about your future. ​This process helps you be more intentional about your career journey by leveraging BSU resources. Education Design Your Educational Journey Your education at BSU is more than earning a degree. It is an opportunity to intentionally design a future that aligns with your interests, strengths, and goals. As you make decisions about your classes, majors, minors, undergraduate research, and other academic experiences, pause to ask yourself: What subjects energize and inspire me? Why am I interested in taking this course? What do I hope to learn or gain from this class? How might this course connect to my interests, values, or future goals? How might this major or minor align with the kind of impact I want to have? How could research deepen my understanding of my field? Who could help me think differently about my academic journey? Take advantage of opportunities to connect with faculty, advisors, and staff who can help you reflect on these questions and guide your academic journey. By being thoughtful and intentional about your choices, you’ll build the knowledge, skills, and experiences that prepare you to become Career Confident . Skills Every experience you choose at BSU is an opportunity to develop valuable skills and move closer to your career goals. Whether you are participating in a student organization, completing an internship, volunteering, studying abroad, conducting undergraduate research, or taking on a leadership role, be intentional about selecting experiences that align with the skills you want to build. Along the way, reflect on what you are learning and how those experiences demonstrate your strengths. Being able to clearly articulate your skills, accomplishments, and growth to employers is just as important as gaining experience itself. Check out a list of career readiness competencies that employers highly value in entry-level hires. By intentionally connecting your education to meaningful experiences and learning to communicate its value, you’ll graduate prepared to share what you bring to the workplace confidently. Consider the following reflective questions throughout your time at BSU: What skills have I developed through my classes, jobs, or extracurricular activities? What new skill do I want to develop this semester? Which experiences will help me strengthen that skill? What skills will prepare me for the career or graduate program I hope to pursue? How would I explain the skills I gained to someone interviewing me? Which transferable skills can I apply across different careers? What stories from my experiences best demonstrate my strengths? What skills do I want employers to remember about me? Experience Learning doesn’t just happen in the classroom. It happens through the experiences you choose. Be intentional about pursuing opportunities that allow you to apply your knowledge, develop new skills, and explore careers that interest you. Consider working in an on-campus job, volunteering in your community, attending employer information sessions and networking events, participating in micro-internships or semester-long internships, studying abroad, conducting undergraduate research, or even choosing a part-time job that helps you build transferable skills. Every experience offers the chance to learn something about yourself, expand your professional network, and discover what kind of work is meaningful to you. As you reflect on these experiences, think about the skills you’ve developed, the challenges you’ve overcome, and the stories you’ll share with future employers. The more intentional you are about designing your experiences, the more prepared you’ll be to demonstrate your value to future employers confidently. Take a moment to reflect on your skills through these questions: What experiences would complement my education? What experiences will help me explore careers I’m curious about? Which opportunities will allow me to develop skills that employers value? How might this experience align with my interests, values, or long-term goals? What do I hope to learn about myself through this experience? What is one experience I have not considered that could broaden my perspective or open new possibilities? Start Somewhere. Explore Everywhere. You don’t need to have your entire future figured out before you begin. Start with one opportunity, one conversation, or one experience and see where it takes you. Try an internship, join a student organization, take on a campus job, volunteer, attend an event, talk with an alum, or explore something completely new. Every experience helps you learn more about yourself, build new skills, and grow your confidence. Some experiences will confirm what you want, while others will help you discover what you don’t and both are valuable. Stay curious, keep experimenting, and give yourself permission to change direction along the way. Start somewhere. Explore everywhere. Build career confidence with every experience. What is Life Design? Life Design is a framework developed by Stanford University that applies design thinking principles to help people intentionally create a meaningful life and career. Rather than believing there is one “right” path, Life Design encourages people to explore multiple possibilities, learn through experiences, and make decisions through curiosity and experimentation. At its core, Life Design is based on the idea that you can design your life—not simply discover it. Imagine Your Future Through Our Process . There are several way to learn and practice this five-step process.  They include: Step 1: Learn Discover who you are, your path, and your challenges. Step 2: Question Create a question based on one of your challenges. Step 3: Brainstorm Share your question with BSU faculty and staff who can generate solutions. Step 4: Act Choose 1 solution and test it in the real world. Step 5: Review Are you satisfied with your result?  if not, try a different solution. Want to learn more? Schedule an appointment with a member of the Career Services & Internships team. Career Design Resources Explore some of these resources to begin your career design journey. Why College? Your college experience is about more than earning a degree. It is an opportunity to discover your interests, develop new skills, build meaningful relationships, and prepare for a fulfilling future. As you reflect, write a brief statement (150–200 words) describing what you hope your education at BSU will mean to you. Consider questions such as: Why did I choose to attend BSU? What do I hope to discover about myself while I am here? What knowledge, skills, and experiences do I want to gain? How do I want my education to prepare me for life after graduation? What role do curiosity, exploration, and lifelong learning play in my college experience? Who has influenced my educational journey, and how do their perspectives shape my decisions? Work View Work is more than a job. It is one way you contribute your talents, continue learning, and create the life you want. Whether you’re exploring careers, completing internships, working on campus, or preparing for your first full-time position, understanding what work means to you can help guide your future decisions. Write a brief reflection (150–200 words) describing your vision of meaningful work. Use these questions to guide your thinking: What does meaningful work look like to me? What motivates me to work? What kind of impact do I hope to make through my work? What type of work environment allows me to thrive? How do I want my work to contribute to my community or the world? World View Your career is just one part of the life you are designing. Your values, relationships, interests, and beliefs all influence the decisions you make and the person you are becoming. This exercise encourages you to reflect on what matters most and how those values shape your future. Write a personal reflection (150–200 words) describing the life you hope to build. As you reflect, consider: What gives my life meaning and purpose? What values guide the decisions I make? What kind of person do I want to become? What impact do I hope to have on others? How do I define success? What role do family, community, service, or faith play in my life? When I imagine my future, what kind of life am I intentionally designing? Odyssey Plan Imagine Three Possible Futures Your future does not have to follow one predetermined path. An Odyssey Plan is a Life Design activity that helps you imagine multiple versions of what your life and career could look like over the next several years. Instead of trying to identify the one “right” answer, you will explore different possibilities, notice what excites you, and identify experiences you can test. The goal is not to predict your future—it is to imagine possibilities. Create Three Different 5-Year Plans Imagine yourself five years from now. Develop three distinct versions of your future. Each plan should include both your career and the other parts of life that matter to you. PLAN 1 — The Path I’m On What might my next five years look like if I continue on my current path? Consider your current major, interests, experiences, career ideas, and goals. Give your plan a title: Sketch or describe your five-year journey: Year 1: ___________________________________________________ Year 2: ___________________________________________________ Year 3: ___________________________________________________ Year 4: ___________________________________________________ Year 5: ___________________________________________________ What excites me about this path? PLAN 2 — An Alternative Path What would I do if Plan 1 were suddenly no longer an option? Imagine a different career, industry, graduate program, location, or direction. Challenge yourself to consider possibilities you may not have seriously explored before. Give your plan a title: Sketch or describe your five-year journey: Year 1: ___________________________________________________ Year 2: ___________________________________________________ Year 3: ___________________________________________________ Year 4: ___________________________________________________ Year 5: ___________________________________________________ What excites me about this path? PLAN 3 — The “What If?” Path What might I pursue if money, expectations, or fear of failure were not holding me back? Think creatively. What have you always wanted to try? What interests, causes, talents, places, or experiences would you explore? Give your plan a title: Sketch or describe your five-year journey: Year 1: ___________________________________________________ Year 2: ___________________________________________________ Year 3: ___________________________________________________ Year 4: ___________________________________________________ Year 5: ___________________________________________________ What excites me about this path? Compare Your Three Futures Rate each plan from 1 (low) to 5 (high). Plan 1 Plan 2 Plan 3 Excitement: How energized am I by this possibility? ___ ___ ___ Confidence: How achievable does this path feel right now? ___ ___ ___ Alignment: How well does this fit my values and Work View? ___ ___ ___ Curiosity: How interested am I in learning more? ___ ___ ___ Reflect What themes, values, or interests appear across more than one plan? What surprised me as I created these plans? Which possibility am I most curious about right now—and why? Turn Possibilities Into Experiments You do not have to choose one plan today. Instead, identify one small step you can take to learn more about a possibility that interests you. I could: Talk with a BSU alum or professional working in a field that interests me. Conduct an informational interview. Explore an internship, job shadow, volunteer, or research opportunity. Attend a career event or employer program. Take a course or participate in a student organization. Research a career, graduate program, organization, or industry. Meet with Career Services & Internships to explore my ideas. My Next Step One possibility I want to explore: One action I will take: Who or what could help me take this step? When will I do it? ________________________________________ Life Design Reminder: You do not need to have your entire future figured out. Stay curious, test possibilities, learn from your experiences, and keep designing. Additional Resources Want more? Check out the following resources: This page contains a thumbnail for an embedded document. View document Open document in a new window This page contains a thumbnail for an embedded document. View document Open document in a new window As you learn, grow, and gain new experiences, revisit these resources often. Your education, skills, experiences, and values will continue to evolve, and so will the life you are designing. Reflection is an ongoing practice that helps you make intentional choices and move forward with confidence. Remember, the Career Services & Internships team is here to help you design your career, so don’t hesitate to reach out at any point in your design journey for guidance and support. Contact & Location Phone 508-531-1328 Email careerservices@bridgew.edu Website https://careers.bridgew.edu/ Address Rondileau Student Union (RSU), Room 104 19 Park Avenue Bridgewater, MA 02325 Office Hours M Monday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm T Tuesday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm W Wednesday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm TH Thursday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm F Friday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm Phone 508-531-1328 Email careerservices@bridgew.edu Website https://careers.bridgew.edu/ Address Rondileau Student Union (RSU), Room 104 19 Park Avenue Bridgewater, MA 02325 Instagram LinkedIn YouTube Footer Column 1 Alumni Connections Blog Events Footer Column 2 Jobs & Internships Resources Meet the Team Privacy Policy | Terms of Service Copyright © 2026 Bridgewater State University"
    },
    "https://careers.bridgew.edu/channels/employer-services/": {
      "title": "Employer Services",
      "description": "Employer Services – Career Services &  Internship Program | Bridgewater State University BSU and Career Services & Internships will be closed on Monday, September 7th for the Labor Day holiday. New: AI Search is live — ask any career question and get instant answers from our resources. Just click on the \"U\" message icon in the bottom right corner. Log in for personalized results. Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Search Keywords Log in Sign up Guests Access Handshake Schedule an Appointment Request a Workshop Customize Your Experience Who You Are Students Undergraduate Students Graduate Students Non-Degree Students Transfer Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents & Families Prospective Students & Guests Affinities/Identities Asian, Pacific Islanders, & Native American Black/African American First-Generation International Students Latine LGBTQIA+ Students With Disabilities Undocumented Students Veterans Women Career Interests Aviation Business, Accounting and Finance Communications, Marketing and Media Counseling & Human Services Education Fine & Performing Arts Health Care and Wellness Public Administration, Government, and Law STEM (Science, Technology, Engineering & Mathematics) Technology and Cybersecurity Undeclared Students/Explore Career Options Career Resources Bear Essentials Career Readiness: What Employers Are Looking For Cooperative Education (Co-op) Program Create a Resume or Cover Letter Digital Content Library Expand Your Network Explore Your Interests / Self Assessment Find an Internship Internship Funding Make the Most of a Career Fair Negotiate an Offer Prepare for an Interview Prepare for Graduate School Professional Development Funding Search for a Job Signature Internship Programs Design Your Career at BSU Recruit BSU Talent Employer Services HireLocally Job Postings/Handshake Career Fairs & Events Branding Opportunities Internship Information for Employers Internship FAQs CUBEs Industry Mentors Program Recruiting Policies Support #BSU Works About Us Mission & Memberships CSI Reports Staff Location, Parking and Social Media Schedule Appointments Vendor/Resource Requests Career Outcomes Employer Services Bridgewater State University has established a solid reputation for preparing its graduates to meet the needs of today’s workplace.  Our services are designed to assist you in your recruiting efforts and to help establish your organization as an employer of choice.  The Career Services & Internships Office is dedicated to providing high-quality service to all of its constituents. By providing many recruiting opportunities to employers, we take pride in contributing to the quest of making business and industry thrive in Southeastern Massachusetts. We are continuously reviewing our programs to provide you with the most effective services to meet your ever-changing needs. The Bridgewater Student Academically, Bridgewater State University prepares students to enter the work force as well as to become leaders in their field. Bridgewater State University as a community sees Southeastern Massachusetts on the rise, full of new and expanding opportunities. With 91% of the Class of 2024 entering the workforce in Massachusetts, Bridgewater State University takes pride in the enormous strides made in Southeastern Massachusetts, as well as its contributions to the vitality of Massachusetts economy. See where the Class of 2024 is employed by reading the Class of 2024 First Destination Survey PDF . Our students are ready and eager to enter the workforce. Many fully finance their own education while working full-time and attending school full-time; they know how to multi-task and work hard. Combined with a strong academic background, our students will make excellent additions to your workforce due to their strong work ethic. Information for New Employers Below are some tips, information and links to help you get started and connected to our high quality candidates at BSU. If at any time you have questions contact our office. Review our Recruitment Policies and Procedures PDF to ensure which programs are right for your organization Create a free account on our Handshake job and internship database: Handshake Employer Account Creation Link Post a job or internship; Review this blog post from Handshake for tips on postings for maximum results Request a site visit or campus visit Get on campus and be active to gain and retain student attention For more information about working with the Career Services & Internships Office to recruit BSU talent, contact Tracey Gray at tgray@bridgew.edu Skip filter options to posts Featured Articles Filter Clear Filter: Filters See More Filters Filter Controls Blog Categories: Alumni Stories Career & Professional Development Advice Employer Spotlights Front Page News Guest Blog Industry Insights Student Stories Close Clear Filters September is the make-or-break month for intern hiring Share This: Share September is the make-or-break month for intern hiring on Facebook Share September is the make-or-break month for intern hiring on LinkedIn Share September is the make-or-break month for intern hiring on X Copy Link September is the real new year on campus. The energy is fresh but not chaotic. Students have shaken off the chaos of move-in and the shock of their first syllabus. Career centers flip the “open” sign to full brightness. Employers  … By College Recruiter Show author info College Recruiter believes that every student and recent grad deserves a great career. facebook linkedin youtube Read more » What are students looking for in a potential employer? Share This: Share What are students looking for in a potential employer? on Facebook Share What are students looking for in a potential employer? on LinkedIn Share What are students looking for in a potential employer? on X Copy Link Article courtesy of Universum Communications 2020’s most valued employer attributes shows a more pragmatic side to students. In rela