    "https://careers.bridgew.edu/channels/internship-faqs/": {
      "title": "Internship FAQs",
      "description": "Internship FAQs – Career Services &  Internship Program | Bridgewater State University BSU and Career Services & Internships will be closed on Monday, September 7th for the Labor Day holiday. New: AI Search is live — ask any career question and get instant answers from our resources. Just click on the \"U\" message icon in the bottom right corner. Log in for personalized results. Skip to content Skip to AI Search Skip to main nav Search Keywords Log in Sign up Guests Access Handshake Schedule an Appointment Request a Workshop Customize Your Experience Who You Are Students Undergraduate Students Graduate Students Non-Degree Students Transfer Students Alumni Faculty & Staff Parents & Families Prospective Students & Guests Affinities/Identities Asian, Pacific Islanders, & Native American Black/African American First-Generation International Students Latine LGBTQIA+ Students With Disabilities Undocumented Students Veterans Women Career Interests Aviation Business, Accounting and Finance Communications, Marketing and Media Counseling & Human Services Education Fine & Performing Arts Health Care and Wellness Public Administration, Government, and Law STEM (Science, Technology, Engineering & Mathematics) Technology and Cybersecurity Undeclared Students/Explore Career Options Career Resources Bear Essentials Career Readiness: What Employers Are Looking For Cooperative Education (Co-op) Program Create a Resume or Cover Letter Digital Content Library Expand Your Network Explore Your Interests / Self Assessment Find an Internship Internship Funding Make the Most of a Career Fair Negotiate an Offer Prepare for an Interview Prepare for Graduate School Professional Development Funding Search for a Job Signature Internship Programs Design Your Career at BSU Recruit BSU Talent Employer Services HireLocally Job Postings/Handshake Career Fairs & Events Branding Opportunities Internship Information for Employers Internship FAQs CUBEs Industry Mentors Program Recruiting Policies Support #BSU Works About Us Mission & Memberships CSI Reports Staff Location, Parking and Social Media Schedule Appointments Vendor/Resource Requests Career Outcomes Internship FAQs Frequently Asked Questions Who Can Do an Internship? Freshmen and Sophomores:  Non-credit experience only (paid or unpaid) Juniors and Seniors:  Credit and paid, credit-only (non-paid), non-credit, paid, or unpaid Graduate Students:  Requires program advisor approval What is The Difference Between an Internship For Credit VS. Non-Credit? An internship for credit involves a site supervisor, a faculty internship advisor, and the student intern. The student must complete an internship application, have the internship site and credit hours approved by their faculty advisor, then register for the internship. Both field duties and academic work is expected of the student. Clocked hours, credit, a grade, and an evaluation complete the experience. A non-credit internship involves a site supervisor, the Career Services & Internships Office, and the student intern. Students must obtain approval from an employer for a non-credit experience. Students are encouraged to keep a weekly journal.  No credit, grade, or evaluation is required. How Many Hours Constitute an Internship? A minimum of 45 clocked hours in the field is required for each credit hour granted in an internship or practicum.  Three credits equals 135 clocked hours for a 15 week semester (fall or spring) or 10 weeks during the summer.  Students may work between 10-15 hours on-site each week. Can Interns be Paid? Yes, many of our current employers offer an hourly wage ranging from $15.00 – $22.00 per hour. Compensation is generally commensurate with experience and varies by industry and demand. Stipends are also an option that employers might consider. An internship can be a paid or unpaid opportunity. Most of the internships that BSU students complete are funded either by a stipend and/or hourly wage. University policy allows students to earn credit for the internship whether it is paid or unpaid. How Often And In What Ways Should Faculty Internship Supervisors Communicate With You On-Site? This is at the discretion of each faculty member. An example may be a site visit, one or two emails mid-semester, and/or sending the employer a performance evaluation two weeks prior to the completion of the student’s internship. How Does an Employer List an Internship With Bridgewater State University? Handshake Login Page – If you already have a Handshake account, please add Bridgewater State University to your list of schools you work with.  You will then make your job and internship listings available to us and have access to registering for career fairs and on campus interview dates. If you are not already using Handshake, visit the Handshake New Employers Registration page and create your company and profile.  Once approved, you will be able to post your opportunities.  For help creating your profile, visit the Handshake Help Page . Skip filter options to posts Featured Articles Filter Clear Filter: Filters See More Filters Filter Controls Blog Categories: Alumni Stories Career & Professional Development Advice Employer Spotlights Front Page News Guest Blog Industry Insights Student Stories Close Clear Filters September is the make-or-break month for intern hiring Share This: Share September is the make-or-break month for intern hiring on Facebook Share September is the make-or-break month for intern hiring on LinkedIn Share September is the make-or-break month for intern hiring on X Copy Link September is the real new year on campus. The energy is fresh but not chaotic. Students have shaken off the chaos of move-in and the shock of their first syllabus. Career centers flip the “open” sign to full brightness. Employers  … By College Recruiter Show author info College Recruiter believes that every student and recent grad deserves a great career. facebook linkedin youtube Read more » Making a Great First Interview Impression as a Recruiter Share This: Share Making a Great First Interview Impression as a Recruiter on Facebook Share Making a Great First Interview Impression as a Recruiter on LinkedIn Share Making a Great First Interview Impression as a Recruiter on X Copy Link You’ve worked hard to source and schedule top talent for your facility. The challenging job market has made it difficult, but you’re confident you have a good selection of candidates from which to choose. As you prepare to meet potential  … By HospitalRecruiting Show author info HospitalRecruiting is an online healthcare job board for physicians, advanced practitioners, nurses, and allied health professionals. Visit our site to view current medical practice and employment opportunities or navigate to our Healthcare Career Resources Blog for more great articles like this one! facebook linkedin pinterest twitter Read more » Page 1 Page 2 Next page » Sidebar Featured Articles September is the make-or-break month for intern hiring Making a Great First Interview Impression as a Recruiter Inside Internships, Chapter 1: What Do Students Expect from Internships? View All Contact & Location Phone 508-531-1328 Email careerservices@bridgew.edu Website https://careers.bridgew.edu/ Address Rondileau Student Union (RSU), Room 104 19 Park Avenue Bridgewater, MA 02325 Office Hours M Monday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm T Tuesday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm W Wednesday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm TH Thursday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm F Friday 9:00 am - 5:00 pm Phone 508-531-1328 Email careerservices@bridgew.edu Website https://careers.bridgew.edu/ Address Rondileau Student Union (RSU), Room 104 19 Park Avenue Bridgewater, MA 02325 Instagram LinkedIn YouTube Footer Column 1 Alumni Connections Blog Events Footer Column 2 Jobs & Internships Resources Meet the Team Privacy Policy | Terms of Service Copyright © 2026 Bridgewater State University"
